 <section class="three-tool-section" id="qadr-meaning"><h3>What does the Quran say about Laylatul Qadr?</h3>
  <p><a href="https://quran.com/97" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Surah al-Qadr (Quran 97:1–5)</a> associates the night with the sending down of the Quran, describes its immense virtue and refers to peace until dawn. <a href="https://quran.com/97/3" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Quran 97:3</a> places its value beyond a thousand months. The text does not identify a fixed Gregorian date that an app can reveal.</p>
  <p>The name is often transliterated Laylat al-Qadr or Laylatul Qadr; it is also called the Night of Decree or Night of Power in English. The calculator answers <em>which civil evenings correspond to numbered Ramadan nights</em>, not <em>which one is certainly the blessed night</em>.</p>
 </section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="qadr-odd"><h3>Which are the odd nights of the last ten days of Ramadan?</h3>
  <p><a href="https://sunnah.com/bukhari:2017" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sahih al-Bukhari 2017</a> directs seeking Laylatul Qadr in odd nights of the last ten. Common planning calendars highlight the <strong>21st, 23rd, 25th, 27th and 29th</strong> Islamic nights, counted from the beginning of Ramadan. They are invitations to increase devotion, not five exclusive guaranteed dates.</p>
  <p>Why not focus only on the 27th? It has an important place in some reports and communal customs, but no calculator, observation of moon phase or fixed civil-date algorithm establishes that it must be Laylatul Qadr every year. A sustained approach during all the last ten nights is more faithful to the broader guidance.</p>
 </section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="qadr-count"><h3>How do the last ten nights differ in a 29-day versus a 30-day Ramadan?</h3>
  <div class="three-tool-comparison"><div><h4>If Ramadan lasts 30 nights</h4><p>The final ten are <strong>nights 21–30</strong>. Odd numbers from the start are 21, 23, 25, 27 and 29.</p></div><div><h4>If Ramadan lasts 29 nights</h4><p>The final ten are <strong>nights 20–29</strong>. The same five odd-numbered nights occur within them, but the ten-night interval begins one night earlier.</p></div></div>
  <p><a href="https://sunnah.com/bukhari:2021" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sahih al-Bukhari 2021</a> uses wording that counts nights remaining until the end of Ramadan. The parity of a night counted backward can differ from its number counted forward when month length is different. This is a further reason to seek Laylatul Qadr throughout the closing nights instead of restricting worship to a single list.</p>
  <p class="three-tool-caution">The final length of Ramadan is not independently known to this tool and may be confirmed only with a relevant Shawwal announcement. The 29/30 selector changes planning boundaries, not religious facts.</p>
 </section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="qadr-evening"><h3>When do the 21st and 27th nights begin?</h3>
  <p>Islamic nights begin at <strong>sunset on the preceding civil evening</strong>. The <strong>21st night</strong> starts at local Maghrib after the twentieth daytime fast; the <strong>27th night</strong> starts after the twenty-sixth daytime fast. A Gregorian calendar event for an evening should be labelled with that evening's civil date, not the following morning's date.</p>
  <p>The planner deliberately outputs <em>calendar-day markers</em>, not precise Maghrib alarms. It cannot confirm the user's horizon, prayer method or locality from a date field. Use the <a href="/tools/fasting-times/">Suhoor &amp; Iftar Times</a> or your mosque's schedule to check the actual sunset.</p>
 </section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="qadr-dua"><h3>Authentic Laylatul Qadr dua: Arabic, transliteration and meaning</h3>
  <p>When Aisha asked the Prophet ﷺ what to say if she recognised the Night of Decree, a supplication asking Allah's pardon was narrated in <a href="https://sunnah.com/tirmidhi:3513" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Jami at-Tirmidhi 3513</a> (displayed as Sahih in Sunnah.com's grading). Wording variants include an additional description of Allah's generosity; check the linked report for context.</p>
  <div class="three-tool-dua"><p class="three-tool-arabic" lang="ar" dir="rtl">اللَّهُمَّ إِنَّكَ عَفُوٌّ تُحِبُّ الْعَفْوَ فَاعْفُ عَنِّي</p><p><strong>Transliteration:</strong> Allahumma innaka ʿafuwwun tuhibbu al-ʿafwa faʿfu ʿanni.</p><p><strong>Meaning:</strong> O Allah, You are Pardoning and love pardon, so pardon me.</p><p class="small">Source: <a href="https://sunnah.com/tirmidhi:3513" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Jami at-Tirmidhi 3513</a>. Presented as a reported supplication, not a condition for worship or a required repetition count.</p></div>
  <p>Personal duas can also be made sincerely in one's own language. This page does not invent a fixed number of recitations or imply an Arabic formula is mandatory for receiving the night's blessings.</p>
 </section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="qadr-practice"><h3>How to plan the last ten nights sustainably</h3>
  <ol class="three-tool-steps"><li><strong>Protect the core obligations.</strong> Keep the daily prayers and responsibilities in view while making a realistic nightly plan.</li><li><strong>Make room for night prayer and dua.</strong> Use time for sincere supplication, repentance, reflection and voluntary worship according to your ability.</li><li><strong>Read the Quran with care.</strong> Small, consistent portions can be more sustainable than an unrealistic target; the <a href="/tools/quran-planner/">Quran Planner</a> is an optional organisational aid.</li><li><strong>Remember family and community.</strong> Support others and give charity according to means; the calculator does not prescribe a special amount.</li><li><strong>Keep all ten evenings visible.</strong> Use the full view, particularly while the month length remains provisional.</li><li><strong>Confirm local dates.</strong> An imported .ics file is static and may need replacement if the first fasting day was announced differently.</li></ol>
 </section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="qadr-example"><h3>Worked example: an illustrative first fast on 8 February 2027</h3>
  <div class="three-tool-example"><p>If the first <em>daytime</em> fast were <strong>8 February 2027</strong>, the <strong>21st night</strong> would begin at sunset on <strong>27 February 2027</strong>; the <strong>27th night</strong> at sunset on <strong>5 March 2027</strong>; and the <strong>29th night</strong> at sunset on <strong>7 March 2027</strong>.</p><p>A 30-night plan displays nights 21–30; a 29-night plan displays nights 20–29. These calendar dates illustrate the numbering arithmetic and are <strong>not</strong> an official Ramadan or Laylatul Qadr announcement for 2027.</p></div>
  <p>All-day calendar markers are deliberately not labelled as exact prayer times. They help people reserve an evening for worship; they do not trigger a Maghrib-time notification.</p>
 </section>
